ush later.\n- It pressures e4 and supports a solid setup.\n- It avoids symmetry that might help White.\n\nReason:  \nN: g8-f6 is a flexible, hypermodern approach — developing a knight to control the center, preparing kingside castling, and not prematurely committing pawns. It avoids locking the position and keeps multiple plans open (e.g., …e5, …d6, or …c5 later). It’s consistent with high-level opening principles: develop knights before bishops, don’t over-push pawns early, and maintain flexibility.\n\nAnswer: N: g8-f6"}
